---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="HTML Memory Tested" description="A current, independently written project note for the former DevAwesome issue 49 URL, with a clear ownership boundary." canonicalPath="/archive/49">
  <div class="legacy-page">
    <section class="legacy-layout">
      <aside class="provenance-rail" aria-labelledby="provenance-title">
        <h2 id="provenance-title">URL provenance</h2>
        <ol class="provenance-list">
          <li><strong>Original target</strong><span>devawesome.io/archive/49</span></li>
          <li><strong>Historical evidence</strong><span>The archived wrapper identified Issue #49 and an embedded January 29, 2021 edition.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Current source</strong><span>The creator's live article still names Dev Awesome Issue #49 among the test's mentions.</span></li>
          <li><strong>This page</strong><span>New technical note written under new ownership on 2026-08-22.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <div class="legacy-main">
        <span class="eyebrow">Legacy link, current note</span>
        <h1 class="legacy-title" data-reveal>HTML Memory Tested</h1>
        <div class="legacy-notice" data-reveal>
          <strong>Former issue not republished</strong>
          <p>The newsletter edition that once occupied this URL did not transfer to the current owner. This page does not reproduce its copy, design, authorship, or editorial identity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="legacy-note-grid" data-stagger>
          <article>
            <small>What the project asks</small>
            <h2>Recall the elements you actually know.</h2>
            <p>The HTML Tags Memory Test is a browser exercise that asks people to name HTML elements from memory. Its creator documents how the CodePen experiment spread and how people compared the tags they remembered.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <small>Current observation</small>
            <h2>Recall exposes a vocabulary gap.</h2>
            <p>A low score is not proof of poor frontend work. The useful moment is noticing which semantic elements never came to mind, then checking whether familiar interfaces default to generic containers where a more specific element fits.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <small>Evidence behind this restore</small>
            <h2>The source still points here.</h2>
            <p>The creator's current article links this exact URL as former coverage. A new note about the same exercise answers that click more honestly than redirecting it to the homepage or pretending the full newsletter archive survived.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <small>Limit of this note</small>
            <h2>Memory is not conformance.</h2>
            <p>The exercise measures recall, not whether a developer chooses the right element, implements it accessibly, or understands browser behavior. We verified the live source relationship; we did not turn a score into a skills assessment.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <a class="source-link" href="https://www.paulfosterdesign.co.uk/blog/html-memory-tested/">Read the creator's current article <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a class="source-link" href="https://codepen.io/plfstr/details/zYqQeRw">Open the original CodePen test <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
